; os — the host's file system.
;
; THE FIRST TARGET FILE THAT COULD NOT BE WRITTEN BEFORE. Every name here gives
; back `(T, error)`, which is Go's constructor idiom and which `Prim.Result`, a
; single string, refused — 19.8% of Go's callable standard library, more than
; every language-level limitation combined (gostdlib-2026-09-06 §4a).
;
; The language needed nothing: `(values a b)` has been the negative product
; since values.md and is eliminated by applying it to a continuation. What was
; missing was the ability for a TARGET to say that a host call has that shape.
;
;   ((os.ReadFile path) (fn (bytes err) …))
;
; `error` is opaque here and that is honest: a program can hold one, compare it
; to nothing, and hand it back. Making it inspectable is sums.md's job and needs
; an interface, which callbacks.md tier 3 refuses. What a program CAN do today
; is test it — see `err-nil` — which is the fallible-read shape maps.md already
; established and enough to write real code against.

(target go
  (module go/os
    ; ---- reading and writing whole files
    ;
    ; The element range is DECLARED — a file is bytes — which is what gives the
    ; emitted `[]byte` and what lets `(src i)` be used in arithmetic without a
    ; refusal (elemwidth-2026-08-27). The LENGTH is not declared and does not
    ; need to be: `len` is bounded at both ends without any declaration
    ; (tables.md §2.3.1).
    (prim ReadFile ((path string)) ((array (int 0 255)) error) expr
      "os.ReadFile(%s)" (import "os"))

    (prim WriteFile ((path string) (data (array (int 0 255))) (perm int)) error expr
      "os.WriteFile(%s, %s, os.FileMode(%s))" (import "os"))

    ; ---- the error, which is a value we can test and not one we can read
    ;
    ; `err != nil` is the whole of what this language can do with a Go error,
    ; and it is also the whole of what most Go code does with one at the point
    ; of the call. Declared as a PRIMITIVE rather than derived, because `nil`
    ; is not a value the language has.
    (prim err-nil ((e error)) bool expr "%s == nil" pure)

    ; ---- bytes to text, ONCE, at the boundary
    ;
    ; `string-of-bytes` in builtin.oro takes `slice-byte`, which is the RETIRED
    ; enumerated array type from before `(array V)` existed, so a buffer frozen
    ; out of a `build` — which is `(array (int 0 255))` — cannot reach it. Same
    ; three characters of Go, two type languages that never met.
    ;
    ; It is declared here rather than renamed there because a text-producing
    ; program wants exactly this shape: work in bytes, convert once at the end.
    ; `string-of` is η over SCALARS, so copying a token's raw UTF-8 bytes
    ; through it would turn 0xC3 into U+00C3; the conversion has to happen on
    ; the whole sequence or not at all (string-operations.md §5).
    (prim text-of ((b (array (int 0 255)))) string expr "string(%s)" pure)

    ; ---- the environment, so a program can be told what to open
    (prim Args (none) (array string) expr "os.Args" (import "os"))
    (prim Getenv ((k string)) string expr "os.Getenv(%s)" pure (import "os"))))
